1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,425
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:02,425 --> 00:00:04,365
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:04,365 --> 00:00:06,305
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:15,320
TOBIAS COLDING: OK, so
let me just recall first

5
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:21,920
that we were talking about
a very important concept

6
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:25,440
of a sequence,
concept of a sequence.

7
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:30,520
So a sequence, the
formal definition

8
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:39,100
is that you have a map from
the natural number into R.

9
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:43,800
So then the image of a
particular natural number n,

10
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:47,660
it's usually denoted
like an, like this.

11
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:51,280
It could be some other
instead of a, it could be bn.

12
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:53,660
Potentially, it could
be another subscript.

13
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:58,800
But this is the most common
thing to denote it like this.

14
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:01,130
But it really is a function.

15
00:01:01,130 --> 00:01:02,430
So that's a sequence.

16
00:01:07,370 --> 00:01:11,490
So a subsequence is
that you have a sequence

17
00:01:11,490 --> 00:01:14,010
and then you have another map.

18
00:01:14,010 --> 00:01:18,690
But this map is not just
any map from the integers

19
00:01:18,690 --> 00:01:19,850
to the integers.

20
00:01:19,850 --> 00:01:22,030
It has to be
strictly increasing.

21
00:01:25,410 --> 00:01:27,690
And why does it have to
be strictly increasing?

22
00:01:27,690 --> 00:01:31,290
Because we want,
for the subsequence,

23
00:01:31,290 --> 00:01:34,690
if you think about the
numbers that come out

24
00:01:34,690 --> 00:01:39,170
when you plug in a, when
you're looking at the an,

25
00:01:39,170 --> 00:01:42,850
then you want some
of those numbers

26
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:44,470
to be in the subsequence.

27
00:01:44,470 --> 00:01:48,070
But you want the
ordering to be the same.

28
00:01:48,070 --> 00:01:52,890
So that's why this map has
to be strictly increasing.

29
00:01:52,890 --> 00:01:56,650
You can't pick the several
elements many times

30
00:01:56,650 --> 00:01:59,950
if it wasn't picked many times
in the original sequence.

31
00:02:03,620 --> 00:02:07,440
So a subsequence
is where you have

32
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:09,500
another map from
the natural number

33
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:12,340
to the natural number that
is strictly increasing.

34
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:16,100
And then you're looking at the
composition of these two maps.

35
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:21,000
So you're looking
at this in here.

36
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:22,780
And you call this here.

37
00:02:22,780 --> 00:02:29,580
And then that's usually
denoted something like this--

38
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:31,780
ank.

39
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:34,980
An is the original
sequence, k means

40
00:02:34,980 --> 00:02:37,940
that it's really that
you take the image of k

41
00:02:37,940 --> 00:02:40,380
under this strictly
increasing map.

42
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:43,300
And then you're looking at
the element in the sequence.

43
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:51,580
So, nk, really, you should
think about it as g of k.

44
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:56,290
So that's what a subsequence is.

45
00:02:56,290 --> 00:02:58,790
And again, it's important
that it's strictly

46
00:02:58,790 --> 00:03:02,990
increasing because you want the
elements to be in the same order

47
00:03:02,990 --> 00:03:05,190
than it was in the
original sequence.

48
00:03:05,190 --> 00:03:10,362
And you don't even want things
to be picked more times than it

49
00:03:10,362 --> 00:03:11,570
was in the original sequence.

50
00:03:15,550 --> 00:03:19,470
So this whole concept of
a sequence and subsequence

51
00:03:19,470 --> 00:03:21,110
is really important.

52
00:03:21,110 --> 00:03:31,390
Then, the next really important
concept is of convergence.

53
00:03:31,390 --> 00:03:34,230
So if you have a
sequence an, then

54
00:03:34,230 --> 00:03:36,490
you say that it's
converging to a.

55
00:03:36,490 --> 00:03:39,190
So a is another real number.

56
00:03:39,190 --> 00:03:40,270
It's not infinity.

57
00:03:40,270 --> 00:03:42,310
It's another real number.

58
00:03:42,310 --> 00:03:56,360
And so an converges to a if
for all epsilon greater than 0,

59
00:03:56,360 --> 00:04:06,000
there exist N, an integer
N, denoted by capital N,

60
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:11,920
such that if little n is bigger
or equal to this capital N, then

61
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:21,640
an minus a have to
be less than epsilon.

62
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:25,440
So if you are sufficiently
far out in the sequence,

63
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:31,640
then everything in that sequence
is bunched epsilon close to n.

64
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:34,000
That's what it's saying.

65
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:50,840
And so if you have a sequence, a
sequence that is not convergent,

66
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:52,990
it is said to be divergent.

67
00:05:03,214 --> 00:05:09,330
And the next thing,
I'm just briefly

68
00:05:09,330 --> 00:05:12,810
reviewing this from last time--
the next thing for sequences

69
00:05:12,810 --> 00:05:25,230
are the algebraic laws or
whatever you want to call them.

70
00:05:27,810 --> 00:05:31,090
And so that is that if
you take a sequence,

71
00:05:31,090 --> 00:05:40,370
if you have a sequence an and an
converts to a, bn converts to b,

72
00:05:40,370 --> 00:05:46,010
then if you form the
sum of these two,

73
00:05:46,010 --> 00:05:52,140
an plus bn, so this is
a new sequence where

74
00:05:52,140 --> 00:05:55,300
the n-th element is the
sum of the n-th element

75
00:05:55,300 --> 00:05:57,500
of the other two sequences.

76
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:01,060
Then this sequence
here cn also converges.

77
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:06,486
And the limit here is
the sum of the limits.

78
00:06:06,486 --> 00:06:16,740
And likewise, if you define
a sequence-- so this was 1--

79
00:06:16,740 --> 00:06:19,540
2 is that if you
take a constant.

80
00:06:19,540 --> 00:06:22,420
So this is a constant.

81
00:06:22,420 --> 00:06:29,460
And you define cn to be c times
an, then this cn also converge.

82
00:06:29,460 --> 00:06:34,000
And the limit here
is just c times a.

83
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,160
That's the second one.

84
00:06:37,160 --> 00:06:38,880
I might have done it
in the other order.

85
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:48,990
But there's no particular
order to have it in.

86
00:06:48,990 --> 00:06:53,810
The third one is that if you
are looking at the products.

87
00:06:53,810 --> 00:06:55,590
So if you define
a sequence that is

88
00:06:55,590 --> 00:07:01,430
given by the product of the
two, then cn converge also.

89
00:07:01,430 --> 00:07:05,670
And the limit is the
product of the limits.

90
00:07:05,670 --> 00:07:13,430
And then the fourth rule is
that if an is not equal to 0

91
00:07:13,430 --> 00:07:20,910
for all n and the limit
here is also not equal to 0,

92
00:07:20,910 --> 00:07:25,470
then if you're looking at 1 over
an, then those converge to 1

93
00:07:25,470 --> 00:07:27,830
over a.

94
00:07:27,830 --> 00:07:32,030
So those are the four rules.

95
00:07:32,030 --> 00:07:34,510
Let me just write it as 4.

96
00:07:34,510 --> 00:07:37,270
And again, it's not like there's
any particular convention

97
00:07:37,270 --> 00:07:38,410
with the ordering.

98
00:07:38,410 --> 00:07:42,390
And I think I might have
exchanged the order of 1 and 2

99
00:07:42,390 --> 00:07:43,170
last time.

100
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:52,690
Now, let's see an example
of how that works.

101
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:04,920
Let's define a sequence
n squared plus 1,

102
00:08:04,920 --> 00:08:10,760
n squared plus n plus 1.

103
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:14,320
Let's look at this sequence
here for each n that

104
00:08:14,320 --> 00:08:19,840
gives us, actually, a rational
number, but it's a real number.

105
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:22,520
Now in order to
understand this, I

106
00:08:22,520 --> 00:08:31,160
can rewrite this one here, as I
can factor out n squared here.

107
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:32,620
So I can write it like this.

108
00:08:44,049 --> 00:08:44,710
Like that.

109
00:08:44,710 --> 00:08:46,170
And then, of course,
I can get rid

110
00:08:46,170 --> 00:08:51,230
of the n squared in both
numerator and denominator.

111
00:08:53,750 --> 00:08:56,790
So you may as well think about
the sequence as this thing here.

112
00:09:00,470 --> 00:09:10,890
So now, I can just first look at
the sequence which is 1 over n.

113
00:09:10,890 --> 00:09:15,690
If I'm looking at this
sequence here, 1 over n, this

114
00:09:15,690 --> 00:09:17,390
here converges to 0.

115
00:09:17,390 --> 00:09:20,150
This essentially just follows
from the Archimedean property.

116
00:09:20,150 --> 00:09:26,530
But we've already seen this,
that 1 over n converges to 0.

117
00:09:26,530 --> 00:09:30,690
And the same holds
for 1 over n squared.

118
00:09:30,690 --> 00:09:33,810
This also converges to 0.

119
00:09:33,810 --> 00:09:36,690
And so if you're
looking at the sequence,

120
00:09:36,690 --> 00:09:42,500
you could even look at the
sequence, let's call this xn.

121
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:44,020
This here is yn.

122
00:09:44,020 --> 00:09:48,700
I'm going to run out of
character very quickly, zn.

123
00:09:48,700 --> 00:09:53,380
This is just going to be the
sequence, which is constant 1.

124
00:09:53,380 --> 00:09:59,060
Well, if it's constant 1, then
obviously it's converging to 1.

125
00:09:59,060 --> 00:10:06,060
So now I can think
about the sequence being

126
00:10:06,060 --> 00:10:18,140
to be equal to z
plus y, zn plus yn.

127
00:10:18,140 --> 00:10:20,040
This here is a sequence.

128
00:10:20,040 --> 00:10:21,860
Here is what yn is.

129
00:10:21,860 --> 00:10:23,900
Here is what is zn is.

130
00:10:23,900 --> 00:10:28,060
So if you add these two,
this is converging to 1.

131
00:10:28,060 --> 00:10:30,220
This is converging to 0.

132
00:10:30,220 --> 00:10:32,940
By the algebraic
property of limits,

133
00:10:32,940 --> 00:10:36,160
this converge also to
the sum of the limit.

134
00:10:36,160 --> 00:10:38,540
So this converges to 1.

135
00:10:38,540 --> 00:10:42,310
Now, I can also
define a sequence

136
00:10:42,310 --> 00:10:47,630
where I'm just adding zn
and then I'm adding xn

137
00:10:47,630 --> 00:10:48,870
and then I'm adding yn.

138
00:10:52,812 --> 00:10:58,310
This here converges to 1, this
here to 0, this here to 0.

139
00:10:58,310 --> 00:11:02,850
So the sum here also converge
to the sum of the limit.

140
00:11:02,850 --> 00:11:04,690
But the sum of the limit is 1.

141
00:11:16,430 --> 00:11:17,250
Put it up here.

142
00:11:17,250 --> 00:11:21,410
And then I'll just go over here.

143
00:11:24,070 --> 00:11:33,230
So now you have that
cn converge to 1.

144
00:11:33,230 --> 00:11:35,710
And let's call this c.

145
00:11:35,710 --> 00:11:40,920
And so, in particular,
all of these numbers here,

146
00:11:40,920 --> 00:11:43,180
these things here are
all strictly positive.

147
00:11:43,180 --> 00:11:45,500
So each of the cn's
are strictly positive.

148
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:47,800
So they're definitely not 0.

149
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:51,720
So the cn, so not only
do they converge to 1,

150
00:11:51,720 --> 00:11:54,440
but the cn's are not equal to 0.

151
00:11:54,440 --> 00:11:57,380
And the limit c, well, that's 1.

152
00:11:57,380 --> 00:12:00,330
So that's, of course,
not equal to 0 either.

153
00:12:00,330 --> 00:12:03,760
So you can apply the
fourth rule here.

154
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:10,480
The fourth rule for the
sequence, for the sequence cn,

155
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:13,600
so applying the fourth
rule to the sequence cn,

156
00:12:13,600 --> 00:12:18,360
and then you have that 1
over cn converge to 1 over c,

157
00:12:18,360 --> 00:12:21,200
but c was 1, so this is 1.

158
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:33,720
Now you can look at
bn times 1 over cn.

159
00:12:33,720 --> 00:12:37,130
But bn times 1 over cn--

160
00:12:37,130 --> 00:12:38,950
remember what bn was.

161
00:12:38,950 --> 00:12:40,570
Here's bn.

162
00:12:40,570 --> 00:12:44,890
And bn is really the
numerator up here.

163
00:12:44,890 --> 00:12:46,890
It's the numerator
up here when you

164
00:12:46,890 --> 00:12:51,970
have divided by
n squared on both

165
00:12:51,970 --> 00:12:54,450
in the top and the numerator.

166
00:12:54,450 --> 00:13:00,070
So bn is the numerator
and cn is the denominator.

167
00:13:00,070 --> 00:13:05,690
So bn divided by cn, this is an.

168
00:13:05,690 --> 00:13:08,310
And now this here converge.

169
00:13:08,310 --> 00:13:10,050
That's what we saw over there.

170
00:13:10,050 --> 00:13:13,530
Using the algebraic
rule, it converges to 1.

171
00:13:13,530 --> 00:13:18,570
This here also converge to
1 by the algebraic rules.

172
00:13:18,570 --> 00:13:22,410
Number 3 of the algebraic
rules is that for the product

173
00:13:22,410 --> 00:13:24,330
it will also converge.

174
00:13:24,330 --> 00:13:27,970
And it converges to the
product of the limits.

175
00:13:27,970 --> 00:13:28,890
So that's 1.

176
00:13:31,410 --> 00:13:36,560
Obviously, this is like when
you're looking at a sequence,

177
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:40,260
it's often expressed
in some form

178
00:13:40,260 --> 00:13:43,940
with some functions like this.

179
00:13:43,940 --> 00:13:46,580
And you do this reduction.

180
00:13:46,580 --> 00:13:50,280
And very quickly, this
would be almost automatic.

181
00:13:50,280 --> 00:13:53,260
This is too easy
almost, this one.

182
00:13:53,260 --> 00:13:54,900
This is the first
time you see it,

183
00:13:54,900 --> 00:13:58,940
but you know in a couple of
weeks this will feel too easy.

184
00:13:58,940 --> 00:14:01,360
Then there will be
something more complicated.

185
00:14:05,524 --> 00:14:11,780
So that's the algebraic
rules for sequences.

186
00:14:11,780 --> 00:14:13,540
The next thing I
want to talk about

187
00:14:13,540 --> 00:14:19,940
is, I want to try to make more
precise this about square.

188
00:14:19,940 --> 00:14:22,470
We made it pretty precise,
but I want to think about--

189
00:14:25,180 --> 00:14:28,380
I don't have a very good
eraser, unfortunately.

190
00:14:28,380 --> 00:14:29,740
Let me just see.

191
00:14:29,740 --> 00:14:33,097
This is a better one.

192
00:14:33,097 --> 00:14:34,680
This one here will
make it very dirty.

193
00:14:38,630 --> 00:14:44,050
So the next thing, we did
define square root of 2.

194
00:14:44,050 --> 00:14:46,270
And we talked about
that quite extensively,

195
00:14:46,270 --> 00:14:50,770
how square root of 2, how
it really was defined.

196
00:14:50,770 --> 00:14:52,410
And it was a real number.

197
00:14:52,410 --> 00:14:57,910
And the real numbers followed
from that, the field,

198
00:14:57,910 --> 00:15:01,350
the reals is complete.

199
00:15:01,350 --> 00:15:08,670
But we also, in the
back of our mind,

200
00:15:08,670 --> 00:15:13,190
we had that we already sort
of knew what square root of 2

201
00:15:13,190 --> 00:15:17,030
were because when you think
about square root of 2, at least

202
00:15:17,030 --> 00:15:20,930
I think about square root of
2, I think about it as 1.4.

203
00:15:20,930 --> 00:15:24,030
Just because you can't really
express it as a fraction.

204
00:15:24,030 --> 00:15:27,810
So you think about it
as 1.414, et cetera.

205
00:15:27,810 --> 00:15:31,140
And then I don't remember the
second, third, and fourth,

206
00:15:31,140 --> 00:15:31,640
whatever.

207
00:15:31,640 --> 00:15:34,826
But you may remember
just a few of the digits.

208
00:15:39,680 --> 00:15:44,560
So that's sort of our
intuition with 1.4--

209
00:15:44,560 --> 00:15:46,920
with square root 2.

210
00:15:46,920 --> 00:15:49,720
But how do we make
this more precise?

211
00:15:49,720 --> 00:15:53,920
And so to make
this more precise,

212
00:15:53,920 --> 00:15:58,800
we want to look at
particular sequences, what's

213
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:02,800
called monotone sequences.

214
00:16:07,080 --> 00:16:10,440
So what is a monotone sequence?

215
00:16:10,440 --> 00:16:13,300
So there are two types
of monotone sequences.

216
00:16:13,300 --> 00:16:16,410
There's one which is
monotone increasing sequence.

217
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:31,210
And then there's, similarly,
monotone decreasing sequence.

218
00:16:31,210 --> 00:16:33,710
So a monotone
increasing sequence,

219
00:16:33,710 --> 00:16:41,210
this just means
that for all n, you

220
00:16:41,210 --> 00:16:45,450
have that the next
element is larger

221
00:16:45,450 --> 00:16:48,290
or equal to the
previous element.

222
00:16:48,290 --> 00:16:51,530
That's a monotone
increasing sequence.

223
00:16:51,530 --> 00:16:54,910
Some people talk about
strictly monotone increasing--

224
00:16:54,910 --> 00:16:57,467
and then this has to
be a strict inequality.

225
00:17:01,290 --> 00:17:04,450
The second thing is
kind of the obvious.

226
00:17:04,450 --> 00:17:11,470
Parallel to this is monotone
decreasing sequences.

227
00:17:17,730 --> 00:17:19,609
So there's these two types.

228
00:17:19,609 --> 00:17:25,050
And this just means that the
next element, so an plus 1

229
00:17:25,050 --> 00:17:28,319
is less than or equal
to the previous one.

230
00:17:33,788 --> 00:17:37,060
And so now there's a theorem.

231
00:17:37,060 --> 00:17:39,530
And I'll prove this
in just a minute.

232
00:17:43,100 --> 00:17:45,940
And I want to come back
to square root of 2.

233
00:17:45,940 --> 00:17:51,060
And then I want to look at
another classical example

234
00:17:51,060 --> 00:17:52,290
of a monotone sequence.

235
00:17:59,400 --> 00:18:01,040
So this is a monotone sequence.

236
00:18:01,040 --> 00:18:03,910
And now let me state the
monotone convergence theorem.

237
00:18:17,415 --> 00:18:18,790
So this is the
following theorem.

238
00:18:24,820 --> 00:18:26,910
And there's two
versions of this.

239
00:18:26,910 --> 00:18:29,890
There's one version for
monotone increasing sequence.

240
00:18:29,890 --> 00:18:32,982
And then there's one version for
monotone decreasing sequence.

241
00:18:32,982 --> 00:18:35,190
I'll just state one of them,
because the other one is

242
00:18:35,190 --> 00:18:37,190
kind of obviously a parallel.

243
00:18:37,190 --> 00:18:48,270
So suppose that an is
a monotone increasing

244
00:18:48,270 --> 00:18:57,640
sequence that is bounded.

245
00:19:01,350 --> 00:19:08,150
Bounded, so this just means
that there exists, i.e.

