01:02:21,690 --> 01:02:24,770
But you can write this in
here, this denominator,

729
01:02:24,770 --> 01:02:29,690
10 to the power 2n minus 2, you
can write as 10 to the power n

730
01:02:29,690 --> 01:02:34,090
minus 1 times 10 to
the power n minus 1.

731
01:02:34,090 --> 01:02:36,430
So in this way, you
can split this up.

732
01:02:36,430 --> 01:02:42,850
And you can write it
as bn plus 1 over 10

733
01:02:42,850 --> 01:02:47,920
to the power n minus 1,
and then times itself.

734
01:02:50,570 --> 01:02:57,850
This is just to make sure that
it's bn, and then to the bn

735
01:02:57,850 --> 01:03:01,140
you're adding 1.

736
01:03:01,140 --> 01:03:06,780
So like that, you
write it like that.

737
01:03:06,780 --> 01:03:16,140
But you see, this thing here,
if you split the fraction up,

738
01:03:16,140 --> 01:03:23,340
you can write it as bn over
10 to the power n minus 1.

739
01:03:23,340 --> 01:03:28,220
And then you can add 1 over
10 to the power n minus 1.

740
01:03:28,220 --> 01:03:31,580
And then you can
multiply that by 2.

741
01:03:31,580 --> 01:03:33,040
And you can write it like that.

742
01:03:41,220 --> 01:03:47,020
But now you see, now I
can write this thing here.

743
01:03:50,980 --> 01:03:55,920
Now I can think about it in
a slightly different way.

744
01:03:55,920 --> 01:03:58,030
So I can write this thing here.

745
01:03:58,030 --> 01:04:02,000
This thing here, that
is the definition of an.

746
01:04:07,030 --> 01:04:13,270
So what I have here is
that an plus 1 over 10

747
01:04:13,270 --> 01:04:17,130
to the power n minus
1 times itself,

748
01:04:17,130 --> 01:04:19,590
but I will just
write it nicely out

749
01:04:19,590 --> 01:04:26,730
like this, that this thing
here, remember the way it goes,

750
01:04:26,730 --> 01:04:30,070
that this is equal to that
which is bigger than 2,

751
01:04:30,070 --> 01:04:31,690
so this is bigger than 2.

752
01:04:35,430 --> 01:04:41,430
Now, 1 over 10 to the n, you can
think about that as a sequence.

753
01:04:41,430 --> 01:04:43,250
You can think about
this as a sequence.

754
01:04:43,250 --> 01:04:45,230
That sequence goes to 0.

755
01:04:45,230 --> 01:04:47,002
This here goes to a.

756
01:04:47,002 --> 01:04:50,990
The sum of these two sequences,
by the algebraic rules,

757
01:04:50,990 --> 01:04:53,190
go to the sum of the limits.

758
01:04:53,190 --> 01:04:55,730
So this is a plus
0, so that's a.

759
01:04:55,730 --> 01:04:58,790
So this thing here goes to a.

760
01:04:58,790 --> 01:05:02,040
And this is the same,
so this goes to a.

761
01:05:02,040 --> 01:05:07,720
So you see that by the
product rule for the limit,

762
01:05:07,720 --> 01:05:12,520
the limit here is, so you
get that a squared here.

763
01:05:12,520 --> 01:05:14,560
And since it holds
for each element,

764
01:05:14,560 --> 01:05:17,320
then it also holds
for the limit.

765
01:05:17,320 --> 01:05:19,520
Except you have to be
a little bit careful--

766
01:05:19,520 --> 01:05:24,440
it may not be strict inequality,
but just big or equal to.

767
01:05:24,440 --> 01:05:29,120
And of course, in fact, it
is equal to because now you

768
01:05:29,120 --> 01:05:32,640
have established the other
inequality that a squared was

769
01:05:32,640 --> 01:05:34,640
bigger or equal to 2.

770
01:05:34,640 --> 01:05:37,180
We had already proven that
a squared was less than

771
01:05:37,180 --> 01:05:38,360
or equal to 2.

772
01:05:38,360 --> 01:05:42,440
So you conclude that a
squared is equal to 2.

773
01:05:42,440 --> 01:05:47,080
If you just think about it,
it sounds maybe a little

774
01:05:47,080 --> 01:05:48,560
bit convoluted.

775
01:05:48,560 --> 01:05:52,040
But it's really writing
down precisely what

776
01:05:52,040 --> 01:05:53,930
you think about
when you're thinking

777
01:05:53,930 --> 01:05:56,970
about a square root of 2
because you're thinking

778
01:05:56,970 --> 01:05:58,510
about the number of digits.

779
01:05:58,510 --> 01:06:04,510
And so including the
first, the 1 in 1.4,

780
01:06:04,510 --> 01:06:07,410
blah, blah, blah,
including that 1,

781
01:06:07,410 --> 01:06:12,090
if you think about the number
of digits, like the an,

782
01:06:12,090 --> 01:06:16,850
then the an is really
defined to make it precise.

783
01:06:16,850 --> 01:06:19,730
You would define it in terms
of this other sequence bn.

784
01:06:25,970 --> 01:06:30,330
So this was just a
little illustration

785
01:06:30,330 --> 01:06:32,920
of how the monotone
convergence theorem works.

786
01:06:36,298 --> 01:06:41,610
And this will play an
important role next time.

787
01:06:41,610 --> 01:06:42,650
Yeah?

788
01:06:42,650 --> 01:06:45,650
AUDIENCE: So the
reason why at the top

789
01:06:45,650 --> 01:06:48,030
we have strictly greater than
2, and then at the bottom,

790
01:06:48,030 --> 01:06:50,730
we have greater or equal
to 2 is because the a's

791
01:06:50,730 --> 01:06:53,380
and the a squared are
limits, whereas on the top,

792
01:06:53,380 --> 01:06:56,260
we're considering each
element in the sequence?

793
01:06:56,260 --> 01:06:59,417
TOBIAS COLDING: So
this here, the an,

794
01:06:59,417 --> 01:07:01,000
you're talking about
this here, right?

795
01:07:01,000 --> 01:07:01,860
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

796
01:07:01,860 --> 01:07:03,995
TOBIAS COLDING: So
the an converges to a.

797
01:07:03,995 --> 01:07:04,620
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

798
01:07:04,620 --> 01:07:06,340
TOBIAS COLDING: Right?

799
01:07:06,340 --> 01:07:09,060
I think about this here,
so I think about a sequence

800
01:07:09,060 --> 01:07:11,280
plus another sequence.

801
01:07:11,280 --> 01:07:14,180
This sequence here
converges to 0.

802
01:07:14,180 --> 01:07:16,500
So the sum of
these two sequences

803
01:07:16,500 --> 01:07:19,400
converges to 0, and
likewise for this.

804
01:07:19,400 --> 01:07:23,660
And then I'm thinking about it
as a product of this sequence

805
01:07:23,660 --> 01:07:26,260
with that sequence.

806
01:07:26,260 --> 01:07:31,700
But it's like each element
in the product sequence

807
01:07:31,700 --> 01:07:33,780
is strictly bigger than 2.

808
01:07:33,780 --> 01:07:37,320
But it doesn't necessarily mean
that the limit is strictly.

809
01:07:37,320 --> 01:07:40,140
But the limit has to be
bigger than 2 because they

810
01:07:40,140 --> 01:07:41,640
could kind of approach 2.

811
01:07:41,640 --> 01:07:43,480
And that's exactly what
happened, of course.

812
01:07:46,940 --> 01:07:51,390
So I also just want to say
that this limsup and liminf--

813
01:07:51,390 --> 01:07:53,790
well, we didn't
actually define it.

814
01:07:53,790 --> 01:07:58,110
And it is how you
define it usually.

815
01:07:58,110 --> 01:08:00,670
But we haven't talked
about that concept yet.

816
01:08:00,670 --> 01:08:03,330
But this, I talked
about with that,

817
01:08:03,330 --> 01:08:05,190
if you take a bounded
sequence, then

818
01:08:05,190 --> 01:08:08,270
you could form two
monotone sequences.

819
01:08:08,270 --> 01:08:10,550
This will play a
really important role

820
01:08:10,550 --> 01:08:11,990
for several things.

821
01:08:11,990 --> 01:08:15,230
This is how one defines
limsup and liminf.

822
01:08:15,230 --> 01:08:17,109
We haven't talked
about those yet.

823
01:08:17,109 --> 01:08:22,930
It's also going to be used to
prove an important theorem,

824
01:08:22,930 --> 01:08:24,229
really important theorem.

825
01:08:24,229 --> 01:08:27,649
And I will talk about
that theorem next.

826
01:08:27,649 --> 01:08:30,430
But we won't prove
it before next time.

827
01:08:30,430 --> 01:08:35,270
So what is that stuff
going to be used for?

828
01:08:35,270 --> 01:08:41,262
And the monotone, what
is another application?

829
01:08:41,262 --> 01:08:43,470
And in fact, there will be
several other applications

830
01:08:43,470 --> 01:08:44,940
of monotone convergence theorem.

831
01:08:49,279 --> 01:08:52,080
Here's a really
important concept.

832
01:08:52,080 --> 01:08:56,772
And so sometimes it's hard.

833
01:08:56,772 --> 01:08:59,200
If it was a monotone
sequence, then

834
01:08:59,200 --> 01:09:02,899
you could write down
explicitly what the limit was.

835
01:09:02,899 --> 01:09:06,600
And in all of the
examples we saw so far,

836
01:09:06,600 --> 01:09:09,460
it was easy to write
down the limits.

837
01:09:09,460 --> 01:09:13,660
But in general, you might not
be able to write down the limit,

838
01:09:13,660 --> 01:09:16,200
but you want to prove
that the limit exists.

839
01:09:16,200 --> 01:09:18,960
And so this is the
following notion

840
01:09:18,960 --> 01:09:21,240
that then becomes really handy.

841
01:09:21,240 --> 01:09:23,130
And that's the notion
of a Cauchy sequence.

842
01:09:28,840 --> 01:09:31,000
So what is a Cauchy sequence?

843
01:09:31,000 --> 01:09:45,120
So a Cauchy sequence, it's
a sequence with the property

844
01:09:45,120 --> 01:09:54,410
that for all epsilon
greater than 0,

845
01:09:54,410 --> 01:10:03,702
there exists N, just
a natural number N,

846
01:10:03,702 --> 01:10:11,850
such that if n and m are bigger
than this natural number, then

847
01:10:11,850 --> 01:10:19,220
the two elements an and am
are less than epsilon apart.

848
01:10:24,050 --> 01:10:26,910
So this is a different
notion than convergence.

849
01:10:26,910 --> 01:10:32,370
Convergence says that a
sequence is convergent

850
01:10:32,370 --> 01:10:36,980
if everything kind of far out
accumulates around the limit.

851
01:10:44,500 --> 01:10:50,540
So this here says that a Cauchy
sequence, that's a sequence so

852
01:10:50,540 --> 01:10:54,700
that if you're far out,
then things bunch together,

853
01:10:54,700 --> 01:10:56,060
basically.

854
01:10:56,060 --> 01:10:58,580
Now, of course, we will prove.

855
01:10:58,580 --> 01:11:07,890
And so we will prove on R. We
will prove the Cauchy sequence.

856
01:11:16,380 --> 01:11:18,330
So we'll prove the following.

857
01:11:21,525 --> 01:11:22,800
Prove the following theorem--

858
01:11:26,460 --> 01:11:28,600
for now, everything is
in R at the moment--

859
01:11:31,660 --> 01:11:48,312
that a sequence is
a Cauchy sequence

860
01:11:48,312 --> 01:11:53,330
if and only if it is convergent.

861
01:11:56,630 --> 01:12:01,030
I won't prove this now, but
I'll prove this next time.

862
01:12:01,030 --> 01:12:05,550
And again, the proof of
this involved this stuff

863
01:12:05,550 --> 01:12:08,030
where we defined
these two sequences

864
01:12:08,030 --> 01:12:09,610
from that bounded sequence.

865
01:12:09,610 --> 01:12:12,130
So it would be useful there.

866
01:12:15,630 --> 01:12:18,260
Now, let me give
you some examples.

867
01:12:25,150 --> 01:12:33,350
One example, and we'll get to
that later, but at the moment,

868
01:12:33,350 --> 01:12:36,590
we talk, everything
is in R. And then we

869
01:12:36,590 --> 01:12:38,530
will later generalize this.

870
01:12:38,530 --> 01:12:42,960
And so to generalize this,
a lot of these concepts

871
01:12:42,960 --> 01:12:45,320
work much more general.

872
01:12:45,320 --> 01:12:49,052
And that's extremely useful.

873
01:12:49,052 --> 01:12:53,600
And it can be used
to find solutions

874
01:12:53,600 --> 01:12:56,600
to differential equations.

875
01:12:56,600 --> 01:13:01,080
But then you have to
abstract these concepts

876
01:13:01,080 --> 01:13:04,200
to where sequences are
not any more real numbers,

877
01:13:04,200 --> 01:13:06,140
but they are of
some other space.

878
01:13:06,140 --> 01:13:09,320
And then you have to talk
about convergence, et cetera.

879
01:13:09,320 --> 01:13:13,040
And so you can
use this to define

880
01:13:13,040 --> 01:13:18,440
solutions to show that various
differential equations have

881
01:13:18,440 --> 01:13:20,286
solutions.

882
01:13:20,286 --> 01:13:23,640
And so that often
goes over using

883
01:13:23,640 --> 01:13:25,610
what's called the
contracting mapping theorem.

884
01:13:33,263 --> 01:13:33,930
Mapping theorem.

885
01:13:39,850 --> 01:13:44,290
And the contraction mapping
theorem have all kinds of forms.

886
01:13:46,810 --> 01:13:51,410
But they're all, it's just
a matter of what space

887
01:13:51,410 --> 01:13:54,530
you're looking at, and so on.

888
01:13:54,530 --> 01:13:57,350
So we're looking at
R here in this case.

889
01:13:57,350 --> 01:13:59,490
And so the contracting
mapping theorem,

890
01:13:59,490 --> 01:14:05,850
so a map is just a
map here from R to R.

891
01:14:05,850 --> 01:14:09,730
And that the map is
contracting means that if you

892
01:14:09,730 --> 01:14:14,190
take two elements in R--

893
01:14:17,290 --> 01:14:22,780
sorry, T is a contracting map.

894
01:14:35,610 --> 01:14:38,832
And this is really
important-- c is positive.

895
01:14:38,832 --> 01:14:40,040
I mean, that's not important.

896
01:14:40,040 --> 01:14:41,920
It had to be positive.

897
01:14:41,920 --> 01:14:45,460
But the important
point is that c

898
01:14:45,460 --> 01:14:47,320
has to be strictly less than 1.

899
01:14:47,320 --> 01:14:50,980
That's a crucial point.

900
01:14:50,980 --> 01:15:00,460
And then it has a property
that when you take x and y in R

901
01:15:00,460 --> 01:15:05,400
and you're looking at how
close x and y are together,

902
01:15:05,400 --> 01:15:14,540
so how close that the image
under T of x and y, how close

903
01:15:14,540 --> 01:15:19,240
they are together, then
those have to be some factor.

904
01:15:19,240 --> 01:15:21,990
And this is the factor,
the c is the factor.

905
01:15:25,760 --> 01:15:27,720
So they have to come closer.

906
01:15:27,720 --> 01:15:30,560
This is why c has to
be strictly less than 1

907
01:15:30,560 --> 01:15:32,320
because they have
to come closer,

908
01:15:32,320 --> 01:15:36,670
if they come closer by a factor
than the original one was.

909
01:15:40,370 --> 01:15:43,670
So this is a contracting map.

910
01:15:43,670 --> 01:15:47,430
So a contracting
map is a map from R

911
01:15:47,430 --> 01:15:52,790
to R so that there exists some
constant strictly less than 1,

912
01:15:52,790 --> 01:15:55,590
so that for all
x and y, you have

913
01:15:55,590 --> 01:15:58,870
that the difference
between the images

914
01:15:58,870 --> 01:16:02,590
is this factor that is
strictly less than 1

915
01:16:02,590 --> 01:16:05,190
with what they were originally.

916
01:16:05,190 --> 01:16:07,770
And so now you get the
contracting mapping theorem.

917
01:16:07,770 --> 01:16:09,710
And we'll talk about
the proof next time.

918
01:16:09,710 --> 01:16:16,550
And again, once you know
the more abstract concepts,

919
01:16:16,550 --> 01:16:18,470
then it's quite easy.

920
01:16:18,470 --> 01:16:21,230
The proof is going to be the
same, et cetera, et cetera.

921
01:16:21,230 --> 01:16:23,470
And it will give you
existence of solutions

922
01:16:23,470 --> 01:16:25,510
to a lot of
differential equation.

923
01:16:25,510 --> 01:16:27,420
So this is the contracting
mapping theorem.

924
01:16:37,520 --> 01:16:47,440
So it's saying the following,
that if T from R to R

925
01:16:47,440 --> 01:17:04,350
is a contracting map,
then T has a fixed point.

926
01:17:08,260 --> 01:17:09,690
It has a fixed point.

927
01:17:13,720 --> 01:17:19,160
And this means that
T of x is equal to x.

928
01:17:19,160 --> 01:17:23,080
And when you apply
this theorem, so this

929
01:17:23,080 --> 01:17:27,280
is the contracting mapping,
and again it's crucial

930
01:17:27,280 --> 01:17:30,140
that this c is less than 1.

931
01:17:30,140 --> 01:17:32,370
That's what makes everything go.

932
01:17:32,370 --> 01:17:35,930
If it was equal to 1, you
wouldn't get anything.

933
01:17:35,930 --> 01:17:38,650
It has to be
strictly less than 1.

934
01:17:38,650 --> 01:17:42,890
And this thing here, if you
set it up more generally,

935
01:17:42,890 --> 01:17:47,330
if the spaces are more general
than just the real numbers,

936
01:17:47,330 --> 01:17:51,730
then this here will be
that you have a solution--

937
01:17:51,730 --> 01:17:53,590
it could be applied
to many other things--

938
01:17:53,590 --> 01:17:56,970
but it could be that you have
a solution to a differential

939
01:17:56,970 --> 01:17:57,630
equation.

940
01:17:57,630 --> 01:18:02,330
This statement here is
like a standard application

941
01:18:02,330 --> 01:18:05,190
to get solutions to
differential equations.

942
01:18:05,190 --> 01:18:07,510
And you can use for all
kinds of other stuff.

943
01:18:10,450 --> 01:18:12,120
So we'll talk about
that next time.

944
01:18:15,988 --> 01:18:18,030
We'll talk about the
contracting mapping theorem.

945
01:18:18,030 --> 01:18:22,130
We won't talk quite yet
about the applications.

946
01:18:22,130 --> 01:18:25,090
Any questions?

